  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mainly dry and seasonable conditions, outside of\na stray shower this afternoon across the western half of \nsouthern New England.\n\nSouthern New England will be stuck between a couple of synoptic\nfeatures, leaving our weather somewhere in the middle as well.\nHigh pressure over the OH Valley and central Appalachians should\nmean plenty of dry hours today with a steady west to southwest\nwind. However, the subsidence from this high pressure will not\nbe absolute.\n\nA mid level cold pool over the Great Lakes and Ontario should\ngenerate some diurnal clouds heading into this afternoon, which\nwill then diminish with sunset. The addition of a weak surface\ntrough moving into southern New England could trigger a few\nshowers this afternoon. Anticipating most unstable CAPE values\nbetween 500-750 J/kg this afternoon. Thus, cannot completely\ndismiss the idea for a thunderstorm or two. However, severe\nweather is not expected. The greatest risk for these showers\nwill be across the western half of southern New England, away\nfrom the marine influence.\n\nDry weather is expected Wednesday.\n\nAs for temperatures, expect a seasonable week, with afternoon \nhighs in the lower 80s and higher-terrain communities in the \nnorthern Worcester Hills and eastern Berkshires remaining in the\nmiddle to upper 70s. Nightly lows will be a touch cooler as \nclear skies and light winds allow for radiational cooling, with \ntemperatures slipping back into the 50s while urban areas remain\nnear 60 degrees. This setup may allow for another night of low \nstratus or fog. Confidence in how widespread it becomes remains \nlow; however, it does not appear this round of fog will be as \nprevalent as last night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Main risk for a round of scattered showers and a \nfew thunderstorms will be sometime late Thu/Thu night into Fri \nwith summerlike humidity. Mainly dry and seasonable weather \nfavored for this weekend.\n\nThe next shortwave trough approaches from the west by late in\nthe work week. Ahead of this approaching shortwave, summer-like \nhumidity will return Thu into Fri. Timing quite uncertain this \nfar out in the forecast. But appears there will be the potential\nfor a round of scattered showers and a few thunderstorms\nsometime in the late Thu/Thu night into Fri time frame. CSU \nmachine learning probs do indicate some low probs for severe \nweather/heavy rainfall potential. But that is really just climo\nfor an approaching shortwave this time of year. So, not much\nmore we can say at this point. Current guidance does indicate \nthat drier/seasonable weather is favored to return for this \nupcoming weekend.",
